        fp: "A commercial developer entered into a written contract with an environmental remediation contractor to excavate and remove contaminated soil from an industrial parcel for $120,000, payable in three installments of $40,000 as excavation depth milestones were achieved. The contract provided that work would commence on May 1 and be completed by July 1. On May 15, after the contractor had completed the first milestone and received the first $40,000 payment, the contractor discovered an extensive network of buried concrete building foundations from a 19th-century foundry that had not appeared on any historical municipal maps or pre-bid soil core samples. Removing the buried concrete foundations would require bringing in heavy hydraulic breaker excavators and would cost the contractor an additional $50,000, turning an anticipated $20,000 profit into a $30,000 net loss. The contractor telephoned the developer and stated: 'Unless you agree in writing to increase the contract price from $120,000 to $160,000, our company will walk off the job today.' Desperate to meet a construction loan deadline, the developer signed a written modification increasing the contract price to $160,000. The contractor completed the excavation in full compliance with the plans on July 1. The developer paid the second and third $40,000 installments ($120,000 total), but refused to pay the additional $40,000 modification amount, claiming lack of consideration and economic duress. The contractor sued for breach of contract to recover the $40,000.",
